<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_activecode_message_parsing" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>ActiveCode Exercise: Parsing a Custom Message Stream</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>This exercise reads a simulated JSON-like text stream representing incoming ROS-style messages and isolates specific sensor payload fields using Python string splitting or regular expressions, without requiring a live ROS2 installation.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_message_stream_format">
    <title>The Message Format</title>
    <p>Each simulated message arrives as a single text line formatted like <c>topic:/scan;range_min=0.10;range_max=3.50;min_reading=0.42</c>. Students write a parser that splits each line on semicolons, then splits each field on the equals sign, to build a dictionary of field names and values.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_message_parsing_activecode">
    <title>Try It</title>
    <p>Run the code below, then try parsing a message line of your own.</p>
    <program xml:id="ac_message_parsing" interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <input>
def parse_message(line):
    topic_part, *field_parts = line.split(";")
    fields = {"topic": topic_part.split(":", 1)[1]}
    for part in field_parts:
        key, value = part.split("=")
        fields[key] = value
    return fields


line = "topic:/scan;range_min=0.10;range_max=3.50;min_reading=0.42"
parsed = parse_message(line)
print("Parsed fields:", parsed)

min_reading = float(parsed["min_reading"])
print("min_reading as float:", min_reading, type(min_reading))

SAFETY_THRESHOLD = 0.5
if min_reading &lt; SAFETY_THRESHOLD:
    print("Obstacle within safety threshold!")
else:
    print("Clear.")
</input>
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_message_field_extraction">
    <title>Extracting the Payload</title>
    <p>Once a message line is parsed into a dictionary, students extract a specific field, such as <c>min_reading</c>, convert it from text to a floating-point number, and use it in a downstream check, such as comparing it against a safety threshold.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_message_parsing_test_case">
    <title>Test Case Interpretation</title>
    <p>Given the line <c>topic:/scan;range_min=0.10;range_max=3.50;min_reading=0.42</c>, the parser should extract <c>min_reading</c> as the floating-point value 0.42. If a student's parser instead returns the string <c>"0.42"</c> without converting it to a number, later numeric comparisons will fail or behave unexpectedly, which is a common early bug in this exercise.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
